THE VERDICT

(A) OUTER-SCALE EFFECT, WITHIN each config (matched-seed, clean)
    delta = loss(this L0) - loss(the reference L0 = inf). A NEGATIVE delta means the smaller outer scale gives LESS fade (less loss).
  config ref (THE 2-P5 DELIVERABLE):
    SMF p5: L0 inf -> 25 m moves the fade by -2.49 dB, against a combined bootstrap bar of 0.82 dB (3.0 sigma). IT MOVES.
    SMF p1: L0 inf -> 25 m moves the fade by -3.13 dB, against a combined bootstrap bar of 2.41 dB (1.3 sigma). IT MOVES.
    POINT p5: L0 inf -> 25 m moves the fade by +0.06 dB, against a combined bootstrap bar of 0.21 dB (0.3 sigma). NO RESOLVED MOVE.
    POINT p1: L0 inf -> 25 m moves the fade by -0.11 dB, against a combined bootstrap bar of 0.61 dB (0.2 sigma). NO RESOLVED MOVE.
  config rapid:
    SMF p5: L0 inf -> 25 m moves the fade by -1.62 dB, against a combined bootstrap bar of 1.17 dB (1.4 sigma). IT MOVES.
    SMF p1: L0 inf -> 25 m moves the fade by -3.19 dB, against a combined bootstrap bar of 1.98 dB (1.6 sigma). IT MOVES.
    POINT p5: L0 inf -> 25 m moves the fade by +0.10 dB, against a combined bootstrap bar of 0.23 dB (0.4 sigma). NO RESOLVED MOVE.
    POINT p1: L0 inf -> 25 m moves the fade by +0.19 dB, against a combined bootstrap bar of 0.64 dB (0.3 sigma). NO RESOLVED MOVE.

(B) RAPID vs REFERENCE at matched L0 (QUANTILE comparison, NOT matched-seed)
    delta = loss(rapid) - loss(ref) at the same outer scale. The two grids and screen counts differ, so this is a quantile comparison.
    SMF p5 at L0 = inf: rapid - ref = -0.55 dB against a bar of 0.70 dB (0.8 sigma).
    SMF p1 at L0 = inf: rapid - ref = +0.09 dB against a bar of 2.72 dB (0.0 sigma).
    POINT p5 at L0 = inf: rapid - ref = -0.14 dB against a bar of 0.19 dB (0.7 sigma). PIXEL-LIMITED: the grids average a point over a different pixel (10.31 against 3.43 mm), so this is NOT a physics difference.
    POINT p1 at L0 = inf: rapid - ref = -0.06 dB against a bar of 0.45 dB (0.1 sigma). PIXEL-LIMITED: the grids average a point over a different pixel (10.31 against 3.43 mm), so this is NOT a physics difference.
    SMF p5 at L0 = 25 m: rapid - ref = +0.32 dB against a bar of 1.24 dB (0.3 sigma).
    SMF p1 at L0 = 25 m: rapid - ref = +0.03 dB against a bar of 1.53 dB (0.0 sigma).
    POINT p5 at L0 = 25 m: rapid - ref = -0.10 dB against a bar of 0.25 dB (0.4 sigma). PIXEL-LIMITED: the grids average a point over a different pixel (10.31 against 3.43 mm), so this is NOT a physics difference.
    POINT p1 at L0 = 25 m: rapid - ref = +0.24 dB against a bar of 0.76 dB (0.3 sigma). PIXEL-LIMITED: the grids average a point over a different pixel (10.31 against 3.43 mm), so this is NOT a physics difference.

(C) BOTTOM LINE for the rapid-as-default decision
    RULE: rapid is supported when |rapid - ref| at SMF p5 is within the combined bootstrap bar at EVERY tested outer scale.
    At L0 = inf, rapid differs from the reference by -0.55 dB at SMF p5 (combined bootstrap bar 0.70 dB); rapid IS inside the reference spread.
    At L0 = 25 m, rapid differs from the reference by +0.32 dB at SMF p5 (combined bootstrap bar 1.24 dB); rapid IS inside the reference spread.
    Rapid differs from the reference at SMF p5 at L0 = inf by -0.55 dB; at L0 = 25 m by +0.32 dB. Rapid IS inside the reference bootstrap spread at every tested outer scale, so rapid IS supported as the shipped default on this scenario.

